    "texts":[
        {
            "texttype":"Online",
            "textpurpose":"Gallery Label",
            "textentryhtml":"Scenes of dedicating offerings to the dead frequently decorated Egyptian tombs. Here, a man sits on a chair before a table piled with meat and vegetables. He extends his right arm toward the offerings while his left hand clenches a piece of fabric that is conventionally termed a “handkerchief.” He wears a kilt-like garment, a shoulder-length wig, a broad collar, and a sash across his chest that identifies him as a lector priest, tasked with reciting spells and hymns during religious rituals. Above him are the remains of four columns of hieroglyphs. The relief, as with most works of art from the ancient world, was originally painted with bright colors, traces of which remain visible today, including red on the man’s face and body as well as the back of the chair; a darker red on the background near his face; and green on the collar, the table, and the lines separating hieroglyphs.",
            "remarks":"MED1_22_WLA.pdf – Day One Cataloguing"
        },
        {
            "texttype":"Online",
            "textpurpose":"Provenance",
            "textentryhtml":"Apparently in an Australian museum and then London auction; purchased by Peter Sharrer; purchased by the Museum from Sharrer in 1974.",
            "remarks":"From accession card. "
        }
